lked about this in  my importance of prophecy video which  I'll put a link to right here so I won't  go into the whole concept but what  Melisandre sees generally tends to  happen it's not that she that what she  sees is false but that it's vague which  means there's a good chance the details  that she fills in won't be exactly right  even if you want to discredit the idea  that real or and the great other exists  it should be obvious by now that the  White Walkers will play in  Orton role in the series conclusion the  first scene we see on the show is also  the first scene where the White Walkers  pop up you don't create a supernatural  race of evil creatures just to have them  hang out beyond the wall and never do  anything with them so they've never  really been in the forefront of things  but they've always been there  gaining strength and getting ready to  come and basically attack men beyond  that we have a pretty rich backstory  that is foundational in the way this  world has been put together the wall is  a big part of Game of Thrones and the  wall was built to keep the White Walkers  out humanity may have lost sight of this  in the thousands of years since the last  confrontation but everyone near the wall  right now knows that things are changing  and that something's going on at this  point in time one of the things that  makes George RR Martin's world-building  so interesting to me is the way he  crafts the history of his world in a  realistic way what I mean about this is  that the history we know isn't  necessarily to be trusted just like  history in our own world it's written by  the victors influenced by politics and  different mythologies interpret events  through different lenses if you take the  world book or the world of Ice and Fire  or something like the princess and the  Queen as examples they're written by  maester I mean not literally since  masters aren't real but from the point  of view of maesters instead of laying  out a true historical 